{"id":2286,"date":"2026-07-07T11:44:13","date_gmt":"2026-07-07T09:44:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/total-solar-eclipse-in-menorca-2026-complete-guide-maps-and-tips-for-tourists\/"},"modified":"2026-08-07T21:44:59","modified_gmt":"2026-08-07T19:44:59","slug":"total-solar-eclipse-in-menorca-2026-complete-guide-maps-and-tips-for-tourists","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/total-solar-eclipse-in-menorca-2026-complete-guide-maps-and-tips-for-tourists\/","title":{"rendered":"Total solar eclipse in Menorca 2026: times, map, and best locations"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><script type=\"application\/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https:\/\/schema.org\",\n  \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n  \"mainEntity\": [\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfA qu\u00e9 hora es el eclipse solar total en Menorca el 12 de agosto de 2026?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"En el municipio de Ciutadella (oeste de Menorca), seg\u00fan el Instituto Geogr\u00e1fico Nacional, el eclipse parcial empieza a las 19:37:16, la fase total comienza a las 20:30:16 y termina a las 20:31:27, con el m\u00e1ximo a las 20:30:51. La totalidad dura aproximadamente 1 minuto y 11 segundos. La puesta de sol se produce a las 20:45:28; la fase parcial contin\u00faa despu\u00e9s del ocaso, pero ya no es visible. Los tiempos pueden variar algunos segundos seg\u00fan el punto exacto de observaci\u00f3n.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfCu\u00e1nto dura la totalidad del eclipse en Menorca?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"Aproximadamente 1 minuto y 11 segundos en Ciutadella seg\u00fan el IGN, variando unos segundos seg\u00fan el punto exacto de la isla. Menorca es el primer lugar de las Islas Baleares donde comienza la totalidad, y durante la fase total el Sol estar\u00e1 aproximadamente entre 1,4 y 2 grados sobre el horizonte, la altura m\u00e1s baja de toda Espa\u00f1a.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfCu\u00e1les son las zonas de observaci\u00f3n oficial (ZOO) del eclipse en Menorca?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"El Govern de les Illes Balears ha habilitado 26 zonas de observaci\u00f3n oficial en Baleares, nueve de ellas en Menorca: Cala en Bosc (desde el aparcamiento del paseo mar\u00edtimo hasta el faro de Cap d'Artrutx), Cala Blanca (del Clot de sa Cera al restaurante Es Mirador), Avinguda Los Delfines y Son Blanc, las cuatro en Ciutadella; Carrer des Tamarells, en Platges de Fornells; el Passeig Mar\u00edtim de Ma\u00f3; y las playas de Son Bou (Alaior), Sant Tom\u00e0s y Talis (Es Migjorn Gran). El Govern aclara que no son necesariamente los lugares desde los que mejor se ver\u00e1 el eclipse, sino aquellos donde puede garantizar seguridad, aparcamiento, capacidad de acogida y respuesta de los servicios de emergencia.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfD\u00f3nde se ve mejor el eclipse en Menorca?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"En la costa oeste, con un horizonte marino despejado, porque el Sol estar\u00e1 muy bajo (unos 2 grados). Entre las zonas de observaci\u00f3n oficial, las de horizonte oeste m\u00e1s limpio son Cala en Bosc, Cala Blanca, Son Blanc y la Avinguda Los Delfines, todas en Ciutadella. Toda la isla est\u00e1 dentro de la franja de totalidad, pero un horizonte oeste sin obst\u00e1culos es determinante, as\u00ed que conviene comprobar el punto exacto con la app Horitz\u00f3 Eclipsi 2026.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfSe har\u00e1 de noche en Menorca durante el eclipse?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"No por completo. Como ocurre muy cerca de la puesta de sol, el cielo se oscurecer\u00e1 de forma notable, con tonos crepusculares en 360 grados alrededor del horizonte, y bajar\u00e1 la temperatura, pero no ser\u00e1 una noche cerrada.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfC\u00f3mo se protege la vista para ver el eclipse?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"Con gafas de eclipse homologadas seg\u00fan la norma ISO 12312-2 siempre que se mire directamente al Sol durante las fases parciales. Las gafas de sol normales, radiograf\u00edas o cristales ahumados no protegen. Solo se pueden quitar las gafas cuando el Sol est\u00e9 completamente cubierto y no se vea ninguna parte brillante; en cuanto reaparezca el primer destello de luz hay que volver a pon\u00e9rselas de inmediato.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfCu\u00e1ndo fue el \u00faltimo eclipse total visible en Baleares?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"El 30 de agosto de 1905. En el conjunto de Espa\u00f1a, el \u00faltimo eclipse total anterior al de 2026 fue el del 17 de abril de 1912. Despu\u00e9s del eclipse total del 2 de agosto de 2027 (visible como total solo en el sur peninsular), Espa\u00f1a no volver\u00e1 a experimentar otro eclipse solar total hasta 2053; el eclipse del 26 de enero de 2028 ser\u00e1 anular, no total.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfY si est\u00e1 nublado el d\u00eda del eclipse?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"Agosto suele ofrecer cielos estables en Menorca, pero no hay garant\u00edas. Nubes altas todav\u00eda permiten ver parte del fen\u00f3meno; nubes bajas en el horizonte oeste podr\u00edan tapar la totalidad. Conviene revisar la previsi\u00f3n los d\u00edas previos, usar la app oficial Horitz\u00f3 Eclipsi 2026 para comprobar la visibilidad real desde tu punto y tener un plan B en otra zona de la costa.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfPuedo fotografiar el eclipse con el m\u00f3vil?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"S\u00ed, pero necesitas un filtro solar para el objetivo durante toda la fase parcial, igual que para tus ojos. Cuando el Sol est\u00e9 completamente cubierto puedes fotografiar sin filtro. Usa enfoque manual, desactiva el flash y prepara el encuadre con antelaci\u00f3n porque la totalidad dura apenas un minuto.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfSe puede ver el eclipse desde cualquier punto de Menorca?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"S\u00ed, toda la isla est\u00e1 dentro de la franja de totalidad. Lo que marca la diferencia es tener un horizonte oeste completamente despejado, ya que el Sol estar\u00e1 muy bajo. La costa oeste (Ciutadella, Cala en Bosc, Cala Blanca) ofrece las mejores condiciones, aunque el d\u00eda del eclipse hay puntos con el acceso regulado.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \"name\": \"\u00bfHabr\u00e1 restricciones de tr\u00e1fico o aglomeraciones el d\u00eda del eclipse?\",\n      \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \"text\": \"S\u00ed. En las cuatro zonas oficiales de observaci\u00f3n de Ciutadella, el tr\u00e1fico se cerrar\u00e1 a partir de las 17:00, o antes si la afluencia lo exige. En el resto de Menorca, los horarios y las restricciones dependen de cada carretera y municipio. Con car\u00e1cter general, las restricciones en las v\u00edas afectadas se aplicar\u00e1n entre las 15:00 y las 21:00, aunque podr\u00e1n adelantarse o prolongarse por razones de seguridad. El acceso en veh\u00edculo a Punta Nati estar\u00e1 cerrado desde las 8:00. Consulta siempre la informaci\u00f3n oficial en caib.es antes de desplazarte.\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n<\/script><\/p>\n<p>On <strong>Wednesday, August 12, 2026<\/strong>, at sunset, Menorca will experience one of the most extraordinary astronomical spectacles seen in Europe in living memory: a <strong>total solar eclipse<\/strong>. It will be the first total solar eclipse visible from the Iberian Peninsula in over a century, and it won\u2019t happen again from our island for generations. Using official data from the <strong>National Geographic Institute (IGN)<\/strong> and the <strong>Government of the Balearic Islands (Govern de les Illes Balears)<\/strong>, this guide brings together everything you need: what an eclipse is and how it works, exact timings down to the second, maps and the best places to watch it, the <strong>official observation zones (ZOO)<\/strong> and access arrangements, weather, eye safety, photography, mobility, tips for families, children, older people and people with reduced mobility, the most common mistakes, and a complete plan for the day. No rush, no fine print.   <\/p>\n<figure><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" decoding=\"async\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/voyagermenorca.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/eclipse-anillo-diamante-nasa.jpg?w=800&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Diamond ring effect at the beginning of totality during a total solar eclipse, photo by NASA\"><figcaption>The &#8220;diamond ring&#8221; effect occurs in the seconds immediately before and after totality. Credit: NASA \/ Carla Thomas. <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<div style=\"border-left:4px solid #d97706;background:#fffbeb;padding:16px 20px;margin:24px 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\"><strong>\u26a1 In 30 seconds.<\/strong> On August 12, 2026, the total phase will be visible in <strong>Ciutadella from 8:30:16 PM to 8:31:27 PM<\/strong> (approx. 1 min 11 s, according to the IGN), with the Sun very low, at <strong>about 2\u00b0 above the horizon<\/strong>. Menorca will be the <strong>first Balearic island to enter totality<\/strong> and one of the last Spanish territories to be crossed by the lunar shadow. To see it, you need a <strong>clear western horizon<\/strong> (western coast) and <strong>ISO 12312-2 certified eclipse glasses<\/strong> whenever you look at the Sun during the partial phases. The Government has enabled <strong>9 official observation zones (ZOO) in Menorca<\/strong>. If you go by car to one of the Ciutadella zones, arrive before 5:00 PM: huge crowd levels are expected.    <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div style=\"border-left:4px solid #b91c1c;background:#fef2f2;padding:16px 20px;margin:24px 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;\">\n<p style=\"margin:0;\"><strong>\u26a0\ufe0f Updated information \u2014 August 7, 2026.<\/strong> In the four official zones of Ciutadella, traffic will be closed from 5:00 PM, or earlier if crowd levels demand it. Vehicle access to Punta Nati will be closed from 8:00 AM. Measures may change: always check official information before traveling.  <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<h2>In this guide<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#que-es\">What is a total solar eclipse?<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#por-que-especial\">Why this eclipse is so special<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#horarios\">Exact timings in Menorca<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#cronograma\">Day schedule, minute by minute<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#duracion\">Duration and Sun altitude<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#mapas\">Official map and tools<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#zoo\">The 9 official observation zones (ZOO) in Menorca<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#donde\">The best spots: beaches, lighthouses, cliffs and viewpoints<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#orientacion\">Sun direction and horizon<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#meteo\">Weather: chances of clear skies<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#luminica\">Light pollution and the Starlight Reserve<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#afluencia\">Access restrictions, attendance, and mobility<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#como-llegar\">How to get there, parking and public transport<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#seguridad\">Eye safety: the most important thing<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#equipo\">Recommended equipment<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#fotografia\">Eclipse photography<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#familias\">Tips for families, children, older people and reduced mobility<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#errores\">Mistakes most visitors make<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#historia\">Historical curiosities: the 1905 eclipse<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#perseidas\">A bonus: the Perseids<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#planificacion\">Complete day plan<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#faq\">Frequently Asked Questions<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#fuentes\">Official sources<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"que-es\">What exactly is a total solar eclipse?<\/h2>\n<p>A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon positions itself exactly between the Earth and the Sun, casting its shadow onto the Earth&#8217;s surface. It might seem like this should happen every month, with every new moon, but it doesn&#8217;t: the Moon&#8217;s orbit is tilted about 5 degrees relative to the Earth&#8217;s orbit around the Sun, so the lunar shadow almost always passes &#8220;above&#8221; or &#8220;below&#8221; the Earth. The three celestial bodies only align with the necessary precision a couple of times a year, and of those occasions, very few produce a total eclipse visible from an inhabited location.  <\/p>\n<p>The Moon\u2019s shadow has two parts: a central, dark area called the <strong>umbra<\/strong>, where the Sun is completely covered, and a wider, fainter outer area called the <strong>penumbra<\/strong>, where only part of the solar disk is obscured. If you are within the umbra\u2014a narrow band barely a hundred kilometers wide that sweeps across the Earth at high speed\u2014you see a <strong>total eclipse<\/strong>. If you are in the penumbra, you see a <strong>partial eclipse<\/strong>, much more common and far less spectacular. Menorca is fortunate to be right within that narrow band of totality on August 12, 2026.   <\/p>\n<p>During the seconds when the Sun is completely hidden, its <strong>corona<\/strong> becomes visible: the faint outer atmosphere of the Sun, normally invisible due to the glare of the solar disk, which appears as a whitish, diffuse halo around the black silhouette of the Moon. It is, according to those who have witnessed it, one of nature&#8217;s most awe-inspiring sights. <\/p>\n<h3>Total, partial and annular eclipses: quick differences<\/h3>\n<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th>What happens<\/th>\n<th>Can you look without glasses?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The Moon covers 100% of the solar disk; the corona appears<\/td>\n<td>Only during exact totality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partial<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The Moon covers only part of the Sun<\/td>\n<td>Never without protection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Annular<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The Moon passes in front but leaves a \u201cring of fire\u201d<\/td>\n<td>Never without protection<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>\n<p>Menorca\u2019s 2026 eclipse is <strong>total<\/strong>. It\u2019s the only type that allows you, for a few brief seconds, to look at the Sun safely with the naked eye. <\/p>\n<h2 id=\"por-que-especial\">Why this eclipse is so special for Spain and Menorca<\/h2>\n<p>The last total solar eclipse visible from Spain took place on <strong>April 17, 1912<\/strong>; in the Balearic Islands, the last one was on <strong>August 30, 1905<\/strong>. In any case, more than a century has passed. The 2026 eclipse will be the first total eclipse visible from the Iberian Peninsula and the Balearic Islands in over a hundred years. This will be followed by another total eclipse on <strong>August 2, 2027<\/strong> (visible as total only from the southern tip of Andalusia, Ceuta, and Melilla) and an <strong>annular<\/strong> eclipse on <strong>January 26, 2028<\/strong>, completing the so-called &#8220;Iberian eclipse trio.&#8221; After the total eclipse of August 2, 2027, Spain will not experience another total solar eclipse until <strong>2053<\/strong>; the 2028 one will be annular, not total. It is, without exaggeration, a &#8220;once-in-a-lifetime&#8221; opportunity for the vast majority of us.     <\/p>\n<p>What makes the 2026 eclipse unique in our islands is that it occurs practically at sunset. The path of totality travels across the Earth from west to east\u2014from the Arctic, through Greenland and Iceland, crossing the Iberian Peninsula from A Coru\u00f1a to the Balearics\u2014and reaches Menorca in its final stages, with the Sun already very low on the horizon. Menorca will be the first Balearic island to enter totality and one of the last Spanish territories crossed by the lunar shadow, with the Sun between 1.4\u00b0 and 2\u00b0 high depending on the point on the island: the choice of observation site is absolutely decisive, as we will see.  <\/p>\n<h2 id=\"horarios\">Eclipse Timings in Menorca<\/h2>\n<p>These are the times calculated for the <strong>municipality of Ciutadella de Menorca<\/strong> (official peninsular and Balearic time, CEST), according to the National Geographic Institute&#8217;s viewer. They may vary by a few seconds depending on the exact point on the island from which you observe: <\/p>\n<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase<\/th>\n<th>Time (Ciutadella, IGN)<\/th>\n<th>Sun Altitude<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start of the partial eclipse (first contact)<\/td>\n<td><strong>7:37:16 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>\u2248 12\u00b0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Start of totality (second contact)<\/td>\n<td><strong>8:30:16 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>\u2248 2\u00b0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum eclipse<\/td>\n<td><strong>8:30:51 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>\u2248 2\u00b0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End of totality (third contact)<\/td>\n<td><strong>8:31:27 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>\u2248 2\u00b0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sunset<\/td>\n<td><strong>8:45:28 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>0\u00b0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End of observation from Menorca<\/td>\n<td>At sunset: the partial phase continues afterwards, but already below the horizon<\/td>\n<td>\u2014<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>\n<p><strong>Duration of totality in Ciutadella: approximately 1 minute and 11 seconds<\/strong> (IGN data for the municipality; varies by a few seconds depending on the exact point). According to data from the Government of the Balearic Islands, totality begins first in Menorca and progresses south to Formentera (where the first point enters totality around <strong>8:32:49 PM<\/strong>). It will be slightly shorter than in Mallorca (where it reaches one and a half minutes) but an equally unforgettable moment. Sunset occurs around 8:45:28 PM in Ciutadella; the partial phase astronomically continues after sunset, but is no longer visible from the island. To check the exact time \u2014 to the second \u2014 at your specific location, the most reliable tool is the official IGN viewer, which allows you to enter any point on the map.    <\/p>\n<h2 id=\"cronograma\">Day schedule, minute by minute<\/h2>\n<p>An eclipse afternoon isn\u2019t just the minute of totality: it\u2019s an experience that unfolds over more than an hour. Here\u2019s the approximate script for Menorca\u2019s west coast: <\/p>\n<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time<\/th>\n<th>What happens and what to do<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Until 5:00 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Arrive at your observation point. In the official zones of Ciutadella, traffic closes from that time (earlier if there are large crowds). Park, set up your chair, locate west.  <\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5:00 PM\u20137:30 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Active waiting: picnic, check eclipse glasses, prepare camera and tripod.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7:37:16 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>First contact. The Moon begins to &#8220;bite&#8221; the Sun. From here on, keep your glasses on whenever you look at the Sun.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7:37 PM\u20138:30 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Growing partial phase. The light turns strange, metallic; the temperature drops; the sea changes color.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Moments before totality<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Baily&#8217;s beads and the diamond ring appear. Keep your glasses on until the last bright point of the Sun disappears.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>8:30:16 PM\u20138:31:27 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>TOTALITY.<\/strong> When the Sun is completely covered, remove your glasses and look at the corona with the naked eye. Just over a minute you won&#8217;t forget. <\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>8:31:27 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The first flash reappears: glasses back on immediately.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>8:45:28 PM<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The Sun sets still partially eclipsed over the sea. End of observation from Menorca&#8230; and the beginning of the Perseids night.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>\n<figure><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\" src=\"https:\/\/i0.wp.com\/voyagermenorca.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/infografia-linea-tiempo-eclipse-menorca-2026.jpg?w=800&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Visual timeline of the total solar eclipse in Menorca: partial phase start, Baily's beads, diamond ring, totality, and sunset, with the fading light of dusk\"><figcaption>The eclipse phases in visual order: partial, Baily&#8217;s beads, diamond ring, totality, and sunset. Baily&#8217;s beads and the diamond ring occur in the seconds immediately before and after totality (8:30:16 PM\u20138:31:27 PM in Ciutadella, according to the IGN). <\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"duracion\">Duration and Sun altitude: why Menorca is an extreme case<\/h2>\n<p>In most eclipses, the recommendation is to seek height. Here it is the opposite. The fact that changes everything is the height of the Sun: <strong>during totality it will be approximately between 1.4\u00b0 and 2\u00b0 above the horizon, depending on the exact observation point in Menorca<\/strong>\u2014the lowest in all of Spain; in Ciutadella it is around 2\u00b0. For comparison, in A Coru\u00f1a the Sun will be at 12 degrees and in Burgos at 8. Two degrees is, roughly, the width of two fingers held together at arm&#8217;s length.    <\/p>\n<p>This has three practical consequences:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Any obstacle\u2014a hill, a building, a tree, a boat, even a bank of low clouds\u2014can completely block totality.<\/li>\n<li>The solar corona, which can be up to 3 degrees across, will sit almost \u201cresting\u201d on the sea: a rare and spectacular image.<\/li>\n<li>The sea horizon on the west coast is, literally, the best possible \u201cseat\u201d, because the sea guarantees a clean, unobstructed line.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"mapas\">Official maps and tools (free)<\/h2>\n<p>Before choosing your spot, check the real visibility from that exact point. These official, free tools are essential: <\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Horitz\u00f3 Eclipsi 2026<\/strong> (<a href=\"https:\/\/www.horitzo2026.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horitzo2026.com<\/a>): an app by the Balearic Government and AstroMallorca. It calculates whether the maximum will be visible from your location, taking into account the <strong>real horizon profile<\/strong> (not just coordinates). Includes a compass and simulation. Available in Catalan, Spanish, English and German.   <\/li>\n<li><strong>IGN Eclipse Viewer<\/strong> (<a href=\"https:\/\/visualizadores.ign.es\/eclipses\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">visualizadores.ign.es\/eclipses<\/a>): times to the second for any point on the island, with the eclipse sequence.<\/li>\n<li><strong>HERMES Viewer<\/strong> (Ministry of Transport; <a href=\"https:\/\/www.transportes.gob.es\/movilidad-sostenible\/estudios-movilidad-big-data\/el-trio-de-eclipses-solares-y-la-movilidad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official information<\/a>): trajectory, duration, and percentage of obscuration overlaid with roads, ports, and airports. Useful for planning mobility. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Government of the Balearic Islands<\/strong> (<a href=\"https:\/\/www.caib.es\/webgoib\/es\/-\/saber-ne-m%C3%A9s-preguntes-m%C3%A9s-freq%C3%BCents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official eclipse information and restrictions<\/a>): with the map of official observation zones (ZOO), regulated traffic sections, and the ECLIPBAL protocol, updated in the days leading up to the event.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eclipse-map-wrap\" style=\"margin:28px 0;\">\n<style>#eclipse-map-header{padding:22px 24px;background:#08243c;color:#f3ead8;border-radius:16px 16px 0 0;}#eclipse-map-header h3{margin:0 0 8px;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.02em;color:#d7a553;}#eclipse-map-header p{margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.5;font-size:16px;}#eclipse-map-header p:last-child{margin:0;}#eclipse-map-header a{color:#d7a553;}#eclipse-map{width:100%;height:min(72vh,720px);min-height:520px;border:1px solid rgba(8,36,60,.18);border-top:0;border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 12px 34px rgba(8,36,60,.15);z-index:1;}.zoo-marker-ecl{display:grid;place-items:center;width:46px;height:26px;border-radius:13px;background:#1f7a4d;color:#ffffff;border:2px solid #cdeddb;font-weight:800;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.06em;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.reg-marker-ecl{display:grid;place-items:center;width:34px;height:34px;border-radius:50%;background:#b45309;color:#ffffff;border:3px solid #fed7aa;font-weight:800;font-size:15px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.3